WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.200
Bond fans certainly can cherry-pick their&nbsp;
favorites. But there's really only one way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.480
to view these adventures as the work of&nbsp;
one semi-ageless operative — and it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.280
in chronological order. You've been watching&nbsp;
James Bond movies all wrong, and here's why.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.200
To start at the character's beginning&nbsp;
means 2006's Casino Royale, the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.800
reboot that served as Daniel Craig's Bond&nbsp;
debut. Based on Fleming's first Bond novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.000
the film gives Bond a gritty origin story&nbsp;
in which he earns his "Double-0" status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.160
drives off with his signature Aston&nbsp;
Martin, and uses his brawn as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.880
as his wits — like when he flings an empty&nbsp;
handgun right back in an opponent's face.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.200
Most importantly, the film refreshes&nbsp;
the trope of a woman who does him wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.040
by having a genuine love story with an unwilling&nbsp;
betrayal. Eva Green's treasury liaison Vesper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.520
Lynd throws real sparks with Craig, revealing a&nbsp;
striking complexity beneath Bond's flinty gaze.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.880
Phoebe Waller-Bridge, the Fleabag star and&nbsp;
co-screenwriter of No Time to Die, told GQ -

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.400
"He let us in a bit, which makes the moments&nbsp;
he shuts us out even more arresting. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.600
he grounded a fantasy character in real emotion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.080
which is what I think we hadn't realized we'd&nbsp;
missed amongst the action and the bravado."

00:01:17.948 --> 00:01:21.600
Quantum of Solace, released in 2008,&nbsp;
picks up where Casino Royale left off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.760
with Bond discovering that criminal banker&nbsp;
Le Chiffre is part of a larger terrorist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.040
organization called Quantum. He also ferrets&nbsp;
out more about Vesper's past. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.320
the Writer's Guild strike at the time hampered&nbsp;
a cohesive plot, with Craig admitting to GQ -

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.560
"We didn't have a script. So we&nbsp;
concentrated a lot on the stunts."

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.560
Craig's third outing, Skyfall, continued&nbsp;
to develop Bond as a character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.360
particularly his relationship with M.&nbsp;
After a botched mission with new agent Eve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.840
Bond is presumed dead. But when vengeful&nbsp;
ex-agent Raoul Silva targets M and MI6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.800
Bond returns as M's protector, taking her to&nbsp;
Skyfall, his childhood home in the Scottish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.640
Highlands. There, viewers see the headstones of&nbsp;
his parents, and their surname confirms that James&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:13.120
Bond is the same character across the franchise,&nbsp;
admittedly one without much of a secret identity.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.840
Bond defeats Silva, but M dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.440
throwing off the continuity when Dench&nbsp;
turns up in the Pierce Brosnan years.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.840
As much as the Craig films&nbsp;
serve as Bond's origin story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.560
2015's Spectre also delves into his&nbsp;
connection to the criminal organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.080
a frequent adversary from Fleming's novels. On&nbsp;
the trail of Mr. White, a character from Casino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.600
Royale and Quantum of Solace, Bond learns that&nbsp;
the Quantum organization is a SPECTRE subsidiary.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.080
He also meets White's daughter, psychologist&nbsp;
Madeleine Swann. The two follow SPECTRE's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:49.520
trail to Morocco, where they wind up prisoners&nbsp;
of a man Bond recognizes as Franz Oberhauser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.680
son of a foster father from Bond's youth.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.800
Oberhauser reveals that his current name is&nbsp;
Ernst Stavro Blofeld, which rings a bell for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.560
Bond aficionados. As Oberhauser, he'd faked&nbsp;
his death, and now he's in charge of SPECTRE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.960
which has an MI6 mole with designs&nbsp;
on a global surveillance network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.880
Of course, Bond foils Blofeld's plan, then&nbsp;
drives off with Swann, leaving espionage behind.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.800
No Time to Die forces him back into&nbsp;
the field as CIA pal Felix Leiter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.960
asks for help in pursuing Safin, an anarchist&nbsp;
and ex-SPECTRE assassin. Meanwhile, Blofeld&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.880
stirs up trouble while in custody, and a secret&nbsp;
from Swann's past surfaces. It's safe to assume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.320
that whatever else No Time to Die has in store,&nbsp;
Bond's relationship with Swann won't survive.

00:03:36.072 --> 00:03:41.440
Dr. No, released in 1962, officially introduced&nbsp;
moviegoers to Fleming's secret agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.040
along with the concept of the "Bond girl" and an&nbsp;
eccentric, power-crazy villain. Sean Connery's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.880
Bond heads to Jamaica to investigate&nbsp;
the slaying of another British agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.280
discovering that the death is connected to&nbsp;
recent sabotage of the U.S. space program.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.560
His investigation leads to a private island,&nbsp;
where he encounters Honey Ryder, a gorgeous diver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.040
searching for seashells. The two meet the&nbsp;
island's owner, Dr. Julius No, a SPECTRE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.160
agent and nuclear scientist aiming to take over&nbsp;
a U.S. rocket launch as revenge for both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:17.120
Americans and the East not recognizing his genius.&nbsp;
Thanks to Bond, his plans end in a meltdown.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.240
Connery's follow-up, 1963's From Russia with Love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.760
further explores SPECTRE and its nefarious plans&nbsp;
as Bond goes undercover to meet Tatiana Romanova,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.240
a Russian cipher clerk wanting to defect&nbsp;
with a decoder device. She turns out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.440
an unwitting pawn in a SPECTRE assassination&nbsp;
plot against Bond for killing Dr. No.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.240
Bond strangles one SPECTRE assassin and&nbsp;
goes toe-to-toe with another wielding a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.440
poisoned spike in her shoe. Romanova comes to&nbsp;
his defense, proving that love conquers all.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.960
Definitely one of the most memorable Bond&nbsp;
films, Goldfinger finds Bond looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.760
Auric Goldfinger, a gold magnate who smuggles&nbsp;
the stuff out of England through the bodywork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.920
of his Rolls-Royce. But Goldfinger has a grander&nbsp;
scheme in mind: Destroying the gold reserve in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:08.160
Fort Knox with an atomic bomb, rendering&nbsp;
it useless and thus cementing his wealth.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.200
To enter Fort Knox, Goldfinger enlists&nbsp;
an elite squad of female pilots to spray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.880
nerve gas, which will kill thousands. Bond&nbsp;
wins over the improbably named squadron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:25.120
leader Pussy Galore before stopping the&nbsp;
bomb with a precise 007 seconds to spare.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.600
Connery told The New York Times at the time -

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.440
"It sort of demonstrates what I believe&nbsp;
is a subtler, more discriminating Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.680
than you have seen in the earlier films.&nbsp;
[…] I guess I'm trying to make James Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.840
grow up a little — in the same way that&nbsp;
I'm growing up myself. Not too much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.680
though. Definitely not too much. That&nbsp;
would spoil the fun for both of us."

00:05:45.329 --> 00:05:49.600
Thunderball, released in 1965, sends&nbsp;
Bond to the Bahamas to recover two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.880
nuclear warheads that Emilio Largo, second&nbsp;
in command at SPECTRE, hijacks for ransom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.920
Swiping them involved brainwashing a pilot and&nbsp;
sending him for plastic surgery, something Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.360
discovers while visiting a health spa for some&nbsp;
R&amp;R under M's orders. Eventually, Bond pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.600
together that the brainwashed pilot, whom SPECTRE&nbsp;
kills, is the brother of Domino, Largo's mistress.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:17.520
You Only Live Twice, released in 1967, showed&nbsp;
Connery's Bond at last meeting SPECTRE leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.640
Ernst Stavro Blofeld. After an attempt on&nbsp;
Bond's life, complete with a burial at sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.480
Bond re-emerges and joins forces with Tiger&nbsp;
Tanaka of Japanese Intelligence. Tanaka suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:34.160
that Bond marry to pose as a local fisherman so he&nbsp;
can investigate under cover. The bride is another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:40.560
agent, Kissy Suzuki, who is all business. The two&nbsp;
uncover a secret SPECTRE base within a volcano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.280
where Blofeld hoped to deceive the Soviets&nbsp;
and Americans into starting World War III.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:51.040
With those plans thwarted, Kissy and Bond decide&nbsp;
to enjoy their honeymoon until MI6 interrupts.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.440
Australian actor George Lazenby stepped&nbsp;
into Bond's shoes in 1969's On Her Majesty's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.000
Secret Service. After he rescues the&nbsp;
suicidal Contessa Tracy di Vicenzo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.360
from drowning in the opening moments, she&nbsp;
drives away, leaving Bond dumbstruck — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.560
spurring theories of "James Bond" being a&nbsp;
codename with a cheeky aside to the camera.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.720
"This never happened to the other fellow."

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.280
The espionage plot is a little kooky&nbsp;
this time around, with Blofeld running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.360
an allergy clinic in the Swiss Alps that's&nbsp;
really a way to brainwash attendees into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.800
distributing biological warfare. But that's&nbsp;
secondary to Bond's relationship with Tracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:35.520
the spirited but troubled daughter of a mob&nbsp;
boss. Tracy, a skilled skier as well as driver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.080
gamely helps Bond throughout the movie,&nbsp;
and the two marry toward the film's end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.040
Then Blofeld and an assassin pepper their&nbsp;
car with bullets after the ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:50.320
killing Tracy and leaving Bond poignantly&nbsp;
holding her behind the shattered windshield.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:56.800
Connery returned as Bond in 1971's Diamonds&nbsp;
Are Forever, which begins with him tracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:02.240
down Blofeld to an underground lair where the&nbsp;
SPECTRE leader prepares for plastic surgery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:07.280
Bond ties Blofeld to an operating table&nbsp;
and plunges it into a steaming mud lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:13.280
presumably avenging Tracy's death, although his&nbsp;
late wife isn't mentioned elsewhere in the film.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:14.400
"Welcome to Hell Blofeld."

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:19.200
As Bond investigates who is stealing&nbsp;
huge quantities of diamonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.080
he learns that the Blofeld in the lair was&nbsp;
actually a double for the real Blofeld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.400
currently hanging out in Las Vegas under&nbsp;
the guise of multimillionaire Willard Whyte.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.360
With the help of conniving&nbsp;
jewel smuggler Tiffany Case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.200
Bond figures out that the diamonds are&nbsp;
powering a space satellite and laser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.040
aimed at nuclear installations around&nbsp;
the world. They stop the destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.520
of course, although Tiffany wonders if there's&nbsp;
any way to get those sparklers back to Earth.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:51.200
Roger Moore took over as 007 with 1973's&nbsp;
Live and Let Die. Moore definitely brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.720
a lighter touch to Bond, but this film's&nbsp;
blaxploitation influences haven't aged well.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:01.040
"Well you just keep on the tail of that&nbsp;
jukebox and there's an extra 20 in it for you."

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.920
"Hey man, for 20 bucks, I'll take&nbsp;
you to a Ku Klux Klan cookout."

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.920
The plot has Bond checking out a heroin operation&nbsp;
controlled by a Caribbean dictator and a gangster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:17.040
both of whom are actually the same person in&nbsp;
disguise. There's a lengthy motorboat chase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.920
a wince-inducing Southern sheriff, alligators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.360
voodoo, and some duplicitous&nbsp;
consent with the film's women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.880
blaxploitation actress Gloria Hendry playing&nbsp;
a field agent, and the tarot reader Solitaire.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:34.880
Moore's next Bond film, 1974's The Man with the&nbsp;
Golden Gun, finds him the target of Francisco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:41.120
Scaramanga, the world's most expensive assassin,&nbsp;
who kills with a single shot from a specialized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.920
weapon. Bond also delves into the work of a&nbsp;
scientist wanting to harness the sun's power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:52.000
to solve the energy crisis. When M calls him off&nbsp;
that mission for his safety, Bond ignores him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.160
learning that Scaramanga is after not just Bond&nbsp;
as a trophy but the solar technology as well.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:05.120
With SPECTRE seemingly gone, the Bond films&nbsp;
of the 1970s into the 1990s focused more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.440
Cold War antagonists. The Spy Who Loved Me,&nbsp;
from 1977, does that with the cheekiness and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.360
excess of the Moore era, but&nbsp;
for some reason, it all works.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.360
When British and Soviet nuclear submarines&nbsp;
vanish, Bond follows a lead to Cairo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.080
where he runs across Russian Major Anya&nbsp;
Amasova working the case from the Soviet end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.440
The two form a tenuous alliance to defeat&nbsp;
wealthy shipping entrepreneur Karl Stromberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:34.640
who wants the submarines to launch armageddon so&nbsp;
he can create a new civilization beneath the sea.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.600
Stromberg likes sharks that devour enemies&nbsp;
and has a metal-toothed henchman named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.800
Jaws. For all its flash, the film also has a&nbsp;
notable callback to Bond's late wife, Tracy.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.686
"Many lady friends, but married&nbsp;
only once. Wife killed -"

00:10:50.686 --> 00:10:52.160
"All right you've made your point."

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:58.960
Blame the success of Star Wars for launching&nbsp;
Bond into space with 1979's Moonraker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.600
a film that copies the basic plot of The Spy&nbsp;
Who Loved Me — with that film's henchman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:09.120
Jaws, now working for a different megalomaniac&nbsp;
who wants to kill people and start over fresh.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:13.200
This time, Hugo Drax, a supplier of&nbsp;
space shuttles to the US government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.920
makes off with a shuttle to transport a&nbsp;
new "master race" to his space station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.640
It's not enough to leave Earth behind,&nbsp;
though. Drax also has concocted a lethal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:26.320
toxin to kill the not-so-worthy&nbsp;
humans trapped on the planet.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.920
The film includes a wild chase through Venice&nbsp;
where a gondola becomes a hovercraft and a full-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.680
laser battle in space. Bond also gets a hand&nbsp;
from one of the smarter women of the Moore era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:44.160
an astronaut and CIA agent with the&nbsp;
eye-rolling name of Holly Goodhead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.920
At least she can pilot a shuttle.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.320
Bond went more down to earth for 1981's For Your&nbsp;
Eyes Only. The agent searched the Mediterranean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.880
for a missing British ship equipped with a weapons&nbsp;
encryption device, meeting Melina Havelock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.600
a crossbow-wielding woman whose parents worked&nbsp;
with the British government. Her quest for revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:08.240
against whoever killed them dovetails with a&nbsp;
secret KGB agent, Kristatos, and a Greek smuggler.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.320
The film includes realistic action&nbsp;
scenes both underwater and during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.360
mountain-climbing climax. But first, it closes&nbsp;
the book on SPECTRE. In the opening moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:23.520
as Bond visits the grave of his late wife Tracy,&nbsp;
a helicopter arrives to whisk him back to MI6.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.520
Once in the air, a surge of electricity&nbsp;
through the headset kills the pilot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.400
and Blofeld, now using a wheelchair, takes&nbsp;
control of the craft from the ground.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:36.320
The agent manages to rip out cables&nbsp;
connecting Blofeld's remote control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:39.760
then flies after his one time nemesis, scooping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:44.640
up the wheelchair on one of the chopper's&nbsp;
skids and dropping him down a smokestack.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:50.400
For Moore's final two Bond outings,&nbsp;
the agent again dealt with Cold War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:56.480
machinations. In 1983's Octopussy, Bond becomes&nbsp;
involved with a jewelry smuggler and circus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:02.240
owner who gives the film its title. She's in&nbsp;
league with exiled Afghan prince Kamal Khan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:07.760
who's actually using her circus operation to stage&nbsp;
a nuclear accident with a rogue Soviet general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.720
There are acrobatic thieves, a hunt in India&nbsp;
that's an homage to the short story "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:18.800
Most Dangerous Game," and disguises for Bond&nbsp;
that include an ape costume and a clown suit.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:24.800
Moore hung up his Walther PPK after 1985's&nbsp;
A View to a Kill, which pitted Bond against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:30.000
industrialist Max Zorin, a psychopath&nbsp;
thanks to failed Soviet steroid experiments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:36.560
Zorin has a striking henchwoman named May Day,&nbsp;
and designs on destroying Silicon Valley through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:41.440
an earthquake so he can control the microchip&nbsp;
market. Naturally, a San Francisco geologist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.840
helps Bond disrupt Zorin's plans, resulting in&nbsp;
a showdown atop the landmark Golden Gate Bridge.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:53.440
Royal Shakespeare Company actor Timothy&nbsp;
Dalton starred as Bond in 1987's The Living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:59.440
Daylights. As The Guardian noted, his more&nbsp;
"nuanced and complex interpretation" hewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:04.480
closer to Fleming's novels but perplexed&nbsp;
fans accustomed to the quippy Moore years.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.440
Dalton is introduced during a training exercise&nbsp;
at the Rock of Gibraltar, where an assassin picks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:16.160
off several agents until the film reveals the&nbsp;
new 007. Defecting KGB General Georgi Koskov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:21.760
says his former superior has revived a spy&nbsp;
assassination program, but Bond later learns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:27.520
Koskov is in league with an American arms dealer&nbsp;
and wants the man dead for business reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.480
Because of the AIDS epidemic, Bond&nbsp;
is a one-woman man this time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.040
forging a romance with Kara Milovy, a&nbsp;
cellist who had been Koskov's girlfriend.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:42.720
Dalton's follow-up, 1989's License to Kill,&nbsp;
finds Bond on a cold-blooded personal crusade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:47.840
against a drug dealer whose men leave&nbsp;
Felix Leiter for dead and kill his bride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:53.760
Della. Before her death, Della wonders why their&nbsp;
friend James hasn't found a steady sweetheart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:57.840
and Felix briefly explains&nbsp;
that he was once married.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:04.160
Because of legal issues, Bond didn't return to&nbsp;
theaters until 1995's GoldenEye, which starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:09.040
Pierce Brosnan after Dalton resigned. The film&nbsp;
starts about a decade before the present day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:15.120
with Bond on a job with fellow "00" agent and&nbsp;
friend Alec Trevelyan, who seemingly dies.

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.560
Of course, events in Bond's life&nbsp;
are rarely what they appear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:23.120
and Bond later learns that Alec is&nbsp;
alive and leading a crime syndicate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:27.760
He seeks a satellite-based electromagnetic&nbsp;
pulse weapon known as GoldenEye.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.640
The filmmakers attempted to update Bond for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:34.560
modern audiences by giving him&nbsp;
a female M who criticizes him:

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:40.000
"I think you're a sexist, misogynist&nbsp;
dinosaur. A relic of the Cold War."

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.200
But some Bond things never change.&nbsp;
They also gave Alec a leggy accomplice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:49.200
Xenia Onatopp, who likes to&nbsp;
crush victims between her thighs.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.320
Bond gets some assistance from a CIA contact&nbsp;
who disconcertingly calls Bond Jimbo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.200
as well as Natalya, a Russian computer programmer&nbsp;
who flirts with getting Bond to settle down.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:06.320
Brosnan's second outing, 1997's Tomorrow&nbsp;
Never Dies, explores more of Bond's past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:11.760
by introducing a former girlfriend, Paris,&nbsp;
now married to Elliot Carver, a tabloid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:17.120
media mogul. Carver draws MI6's attention after&nbsp;
a spurious signal from one of his company's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:22.240
communication satellites occurs at the same&nbsp;
time a British warship sinks in Chinese waters.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:26.240
Paris helps Bond infiltrate her husband's&nbsp;
headquarters for old times' sake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:28.320
for which Carver has her killed.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:34.080
Carver has a more unusual scheme for a Bond&nbsp;
villain: He wants to secure exclusive broadcasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:38.240
rights in China in exchange for starting&nbsp;
a war between the British and the Chinese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:43.680
Fortunately, Bond finds an ally in Wai Lin,&nbsp;
a Chinese agent who can handle herself.

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:50.720
1999's The World Is Not Enough packed&nbsp;
an impressive twist. At M's request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:55.040
Bond retrieves money from a Swiss banker&nbsp;
for British oil tycoon Sir Robert King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.720
a particular sum that turns out&nbsp;
to be treated with an explosive.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:03.360
Bond deduces that someone wanted to send&nbsp;
a message by killing King through the cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.240
because King's daughter Elektra had recently&nbsp;
been ransomed for the same amount of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:12.640
Although he presumes that the terrorist&nbsp;
who kidnapped Elektra is responsible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:18.560
King's killer turns out to be Elektra herself.&nbsp;
She's developed feelings for her kidnapper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:23.040
and the two want to detonate a nuclear&nbsp;
explosion in Istanbul, leaving the King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:28.480
pipeline the only viable route for extracting&nbsp;
petroleum from that region. Along to diffuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:34.400
the situation is a nuclear physicist with the&nbsp;
eye-roll inducing name of Dr. Christmas Jones.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.240
Brosnan's tenure as Bond finished&nbsp;
with 2002's Die Another Day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:46.320
Released the same year as the 40th anniversary of&nbsp;
Dr. No, the film pays homage in some ways to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:53.040
previous Bond films and Fleming's novels. One such&nbsp;
nod? Having Bond pick up the book A Field Guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:59.440
to Birds of the West Indies, whose author James&nbsp;
Bond inspired the name of Fleming's famous spy.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:04.480
The plot mirrors some elements of previous&nbsp;
entries with an adversary whom Bond thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:09.840
was dead assuming a new identity to build a&nbsp;
space laser out of diamonds. NSA agent Jinx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:16.400
Johnson teams up with Bond in a story that becomes&nbsp;
more fantastical than some fans liked. Bond surfs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:23.600
through an iceberg tsunami, there's an ice palace,&nbsp;
and the Aston Martin Vanquish becomes invisible.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.480
Although he felt shocked&nbsp;
over being replaced at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:32.320
Brosnan in 2020 said he has, quote, "no regret"&nbsp;
over his 007 days, telling The Guardian -

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:37.520
"Bond is the gift that keeps giving and&nbsp;
has allowed me to have a wonderful career.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:41.200
Once you're branded as a&nbsp;
Bond, it's with you forever."

